from common import *
src('igeeks-v3'); dom('react')
topic('Components & Props')
M('b','k',"What does JSX stand for?","JavaScript XML","JavaScript Extension","Java Syntax Extension","JSON XML","JSX is a syntax extension that lets you write markup inside JavaScript. A build tool turns it into JavaScript calls.")
M('b','k',"Why must a React component name start with a capital letter?","Lowercase names are treated as built-in HTML elements","Capital letters make it run faster","It is only a style convention with no effect","Lowercase names are reserved for hooks","<Card /> refers to a component, while <card /> would be treated as an HTML tag.")
M('b','k',"Which attribute is used in JSX to set a CSS class?","className","class","cssClass","classList","class is a reserved word in JavaScript, so JSX uses className.")
M('b','k',"How does a parent component pass data to a child?","Through props","Through global variables only","Through the DOM","Through return values","Props are attributes on the child element, for example <Greeting name='Asha' />.")
M('b','k',"How can a child component change the props it receives?","It cannot: props are read-only","By assigning to props.name","By calling setProps","With the useProps hook","Props flow down and must not be modified. To change data the parent passes a function or updates its own state.")
M('b','k',"How do you embed a JavaScript expression inside JSX?","Wrap it in curly braces { }","Wrap it in double quotes","Wrap it in <% %>","Wrap it in ${ }","<p>{user.name}</p> evaluates the expression and renders the result.")
M('b','k',"How can a component return several sibling elements without adding an extra DOM node?","Wrap them in a Fragment <>...</>","Return them in an array without keys","Wrap them in a div","It is not possible","A Fragment groups elements without creating an element in the DOM.")
topic('Hooks')
M('b','k',"Which hook adds state to a function component?","useState","useEffect","useRef","useContext","useState returns the current value and a function to update it.")
M('b','k',"What does useState return?","An array with the current value and a setter function","An object with get and set methods","Only the current value","A promise","const [count, setCount] = useState(0) destructures that array.")
M('b','k',"Which hook runs code after render, such as fetching data or subscribing?","useEffect","useState","useMemo","useReducer","useEffect is for side effects that should happen after React updates the screen.")
M('b','k',"Where may hooks be called?","At the top level of a component or custom hook","Inside loops and conditions","Inside regular event handlers","Inside class component methods","Hooks rely on being called in the same order every render, so they cannot be placed in conditions or loops.")
topic('Forms & Events')
M('b','k',"How do you attach a click handler to a button in JSX?","<button onClick={handleClick}>","<button onclick='handleClick()'>","<button click={handleClick}>","<button on-click={handleClick}>","React uses camelCase event props and expects a function, not a string.")
M('b','k',"What makes an input a controlled component?","Its value comes from state and changes go through onChange","It uses a ref to read its value","It has no value attribute","It is disabled","In a controlled input React state is the single source of truth for what the field shows.")
topic('Rendering & Performance')
M('b','k',"Why does each item rendered from an array with map need a key?","It lets React tell which items changed, were added or removed","It sets the item's id attribute in the DOM","It makes the list sort itself","It prevents the list from rendering twice","Keys give list items a stable identity during re-rendering.")
M('b','k',"What is the virtual DOM?","A lightweight in-memory description of the UI that React compares to find what to update","A second browser window","A copy of the database","A faster replacement for HTML","React renders to this description, compares it with the previous one and then updates only the real DOM parts that changed.")
M('b','k',"What causes a React component to re-render?","A change in its state or props (or a parent re-rendering)","Every second automatically","Only a page reload","Calling console.log","State updates and new props trigger a render. A parent render also renders its children by default.")
M('b','k',"What does {isLoggedIn && <Dashboard />} render when isLoggedIn is false?","Nothing","The text false","An error","An empty Dashboard","false is not rendered, so && is a common way to show something conditionally. (Beware of 0, which does render.)")
topic('State & Data Flow')
M('b','k',"Which call renders a React 18 app into the page?","createRoot(container).render(<App />)","ReactDOM.mount(<App />)","document.render(<App />)","React.start(<App />)","React 18 introduced createRoot from react-dom/client.")
M('b','k',"A list of names is stored in an array called names. Which expression renders them as list items?","names.map(n => <li key={n}>{n}</li>)","names.forEach(n => <li>{n}</li>)","for (n of names) <li>{n}</li>","names.render('li')","map returns a new array of elements, which JSX can render. forEach returns undefined.")
M('b','k',"Which statement about state is true?","Updating state asks React to re-render the component","Changing a normal variable always re-renders the component","State is shared by every component automatically","State can only hold numbers","A plain local variable does not trigger a render. Only a state setter does.")
topic('Hooks')
M('i','k',"When does useEffect(() => {...}, []) run?","Once after the first render","After every render","Before the first render","Only when the component unmounts","An empty dependency array means the effect has no dependencies, so it runs after mount only. (In development Strict Mode runs it twice on purpose.)")
M('i','k',"When does the cleanup function returned from an effect run?","Before the effect runs again and when the component unmounts","Only when the page closes","Immediately after the effect starts","Never automatically","Cleanup removes subscriptions, timers or listeners from the previous effect run.")
M('i','k',"What does useRef give you?","A mutable value that persists across renders without causing a re-render when changed","A state value that triggers renders","A way to read props","A faster version of useState","ref.current can be changed freely. It is often used for DOM nodes, timers and previous values.")
M('i','k',"What is useMemo used for?","Caching the result of an expensive calculation between renders","Storing state","Fetching data","Creating refs","useMemo recalculates only when its dependencies change. It is a performance hint, not a guarantee.")
M('i','k',"What is useCallback used for?","Keeping the same function identity between renders unless dependencies change","Calling a function after render","Delaying a function by a timeout","Converting callbacks to promises","A stable function reference helps when it is passed to a memoised child or used as an effect dependency.")
M('i','o',"A button handler calls setCount(count + 1) three times in a row, starting from count = 0. What is count after the next render?","1","3","0","Undefined","Each call uses the same count value (0) from that render, so all three set it to 1. setCount(c => c + 1) three times would give 3.")
M('i','k',"What does useEffect(() => {...}) with no dependency array do?","Runs after every render","Runs once","Never runs","Runs only on unmount","Without an array React has no dependencies to compare, so the effect runs after each render.")
M('i','k',"When is useReducer a better fit than useState?","When state has several related values or complex transition logic","When the state is a single boolean","When you do not need re-renders","When you want to avoid props","A reducer centralises update logic in one function and makes transitions easier to test.")
M('i','k',"Which useState call avoids running an expensive calculation on every render?","useState(() => computeInitial())","useState(computeInitial())","useState(useMemo(computeInitial))","useState.lazy(computeInitial)","Passing a function makes React call it only for the initial state. Passing computeInitial() calls it on every render.")
topic('State & Data Flow')
M('i','k',"What is meant by lifting state up?","Moving state to the closest common parent so siblings can share it","Putting state in a global variable","Moving state into a ref","Storing state on the server","The parent owns the state and passes values and setters down as props.")
M('i','k',"Why should you not modify state directly, for example by calling push on a state array?","React compares references and will not see a change in the same array","JavaScript forbids it","It deletes the array","It causes a syntax error","Create a new array, for example setItems([...items, x]), so React detects the update.")
M('i','k',"What problem does Context solve?","Passing data through many component levels without prop drilling","Making components faster","Storing data on the server","Managing the browser history","Context makes a value available to any descendant that reads it with useContext.")
M('i','k',"What is the children prop?","The content placed between a component's opening and closing tags","An array of child components defined in state","A list of DOM nodes","The component's return value","<Card><p>Hi</p></Card> passes <p>Hi</p> as props.children, which Card can render anywhere.")
topic('Rendering & Performance')
M('i','k',"What does React.memo do?","Skips re-rendering a component when its props are shallowly equal","Stores a component's state","Caches API responses","Prevents a component from unmounting","React.memo(Component) compares props with the previous render and reuses the last result if nothing changed.")
M('i','k',"Why is the array index a poor key for a list whose items can be reordered?","State and DOM can end up attached to the wrong items","Indexes are too long","Keys must be strings","It causes a syntax error","With index keys, React matches by position, so reordering mismatches item identity. Use a stable unique id.")
M('i','k',"What does React Strict Mode do in development?","Runs some functions twice to help reveal impure code and missing cleanup","Makes the app run faster","Blocks network requests","Minifies the code","The extra render and effect run only happen in development builds.")
topic('Forms & Events')
M('i','k',"How do you stop a browser from reloading the page when a form is submitted in React?","Call event.preventDefault() in the onSubmit handler","Return false from the component","Remove the form tag","Set method='none'","The default behaviour of a submitted form is navigation. preventDefault cancels it so React can handle the data.")
M('i','k',"What is an uncontrolled input?","An input whose value is read from the DOM, usually through a ref","An input that cannot be changed","An input without a name","An input with value and onChange","Uncontrolled inputs keep their own state in the DOM. You read it when needed.")
M('i','k',"What is wrong with calling a hook inside an if statement?","The order of hooks can change between renders, which breaks React's tracking","Hooks cannot use conditions inside them","It runs the hook twice","Nothing, it is allowed","React identifies hooks by call order, so the same hooks must be called on every render.")
topic('Rendering & Performance')
M('a','k',"A component renders <Form /> in one branch and <Panel /> at the same position in another. What happens to the Form state when the branch changes?","It is destroyed because a different element type is rendered at that position","It is preserved and given to Panel","It moves to the parent","It is saved in the URL","Reconciliation unmounts a subtree when the element type at a position changes, which discards its state.")
M('a','k',"What happens when you change the key of a component?","React treats it as a new instance and resets its state","Nothing, keys are only for lists","The component renders twice","The key is copied to its children","A different key means a different identity, so the old instance is unmounted and a fresh one is created.")
M('a','k',"How does useLayoutEffect differ from useEffect?","It runs synchronously after DOM updates but before the browser paints","It runs on the server","It runs only on unmount","It never runs on the first render","useLayoutEffect is for measuring the DOM and updating before paint. It can block painting, so use it sparingly.")
M('a','s',"An effect reads a state value but its dependency array is [] , so it always logs the first value. What is this called?","A stale closure","A memory leak","A race condition","Hydration mismatch","The effect function captured the value from the render in which it was created. Add the value to the dependencies, or use a functional update or a ref.")
M('a','k',"What is useTransition for?","Marking a state update as non-urgent so the UI stays responsive","Animating CSS transitions","Delaying a render by a fixed time","Switching between routes","Updates wrapped in startTransition can be interrupted by urgent updates such as typing.")
M('a','k',"What does useDeferredValue do?","Gives a version of a value that may lag behind during urgent updates","Delays a network request","Debounces a function by 300 ms","Stores a value in local storage","The deferred value updates later at low priority, so a heavy list can lag while the input stays responsive.")
M('a','k',"What is required to show a fallback while a React.lazy component is loading?","Wrap it in <Suspense fallback={...}>","Add a loading prop to the component","Use useEffect","Add a try/catch","Suspense displays the fallback until the lazy component has loaded.")
M('a','k',"Which errors does an error boundary NOT catch?","Errors thrown inside event handlers","Errors thrown while rendering","Errors in lifecycle methods","Errors in constructors of children","Error boundaries cover rendering, lifecycle methods and constructors of the tree below them. Handle event handler errors with try/catch.")
M('a','k',"What does automatic batching in React 18 mean?","Several state updates in the same event, timeout or promise are grouped into one render","All components render in one pass","Network requests are grouped","Effects run in a batch on unmount","Before React 18, updates outside React events were not batched. Now they are, when using createRoot.")
M('a','k',"What does flushSync do?","Forces React to apply the enclosed state updates to the DOM immediately","Synchronises state across tabs","Waits for all effects to finish","Flushes the browser cache","It opts out of batching for that update. It can hurt performance, so use it only when you must read the DOM straight after.")
M('a','s',"A child wrapped in React.memo still re-renders every time, and the parent passes style={{ color: 'red' }}. Why?","A new object is created on each render, so the prop is never shallowly equal","memo does not work with style props","The parent must also use memo","Objects cannot be props","Shallow comparison uses reference equality. Define the object outside the component or memoise it with useMemo.")
M('a','k',"What is the purpose of forwardRef together with useImperativeHandle?","To expose a limited set of methods to a parent through a ref","To pass state to children","To share context","To pre-render a component","useImperativeHandle customises the value that a parent receives in its ref, for example focus() or reset().")
M('a','k',"What is true about events from a component rendered through createPortal?","They bubble through the React tree to the portal's React parent","They only bubble through the DOM parent","They do not bubble","They are duplicated","Portals change where DOM nodes are placed, but React events still follow the component tree.")
M('a','k',"What does useId provide?","A unique ID that is stable between server and client rendering","A database primary key","A random number for each render","The component's name","useId is meant for accessibility attributes such as htmlFor and avoids hydration mismatches caused by random ids.")
M('a','k',"A context provider passes value={{ user, theme }}. Why can this cause extra re-renders?","A new object each render makes every consumer re-render","Context values must be strings","Providers cannot hold objects","The consumer hooks run twice","Consumers re-render when the value changes by reference. Memoise the object with useMemo.")
M('a','k',"What does useSyncExternalStore help with?","Reading an external store safely so the UI never shows tearing during concurrent rendering","Syncing two components' state","Storing data in localStorage","Server-side data fetching","It subscribes to a store outside React and guarantees consistent reads across concurrent renders.")
M('a','k',"What typically causes a hydration mismatch warning?","The server-rendered HTML differs from what the client renders first","The page loads too slowly","Too many components","A missing key","During hydration React expects the same markup. Using Date.now(), Math.random() or window checks in render are common causes.")
M('a','s',"A search box fires a fetch on every keystroke inside useEffect and sometimes an older response overwrites a newer one. What is a correct fix?","Ignore or abort the previous request in the effect cleanup","Add more dependencies","Move the fetch outside the component","Use useRef instead of state","Cleanup runs before the next effect, so you can set a cancelled flag or call AbortController.abort() to drop stale responses.")
M('a','k',"Which hooks cannot be used in a React Server Component?","useState and useEffect","Hooks that start with use are all allowed","Only useContext","Only custom hooks","Server Components render once on the server with no state, effects or browser APIs. Interactive parts belong in Client Components.")
M('a','k',"Why can a stale value appear when an interval set in useEffect calls setCount(count + 1)?","The callback closes over the count from the render that created the interval","setInterval is not supported in React","State cannot change inside timers","Effects cannot read state","Use setCount(c => c + 1), which does not depend on the captured value.")
topic('Hooks')
M('i','k',"What is the purpose of the dependency array passed to useEffect?","React re-runs the effect only when one of the listed values has changed","It lists the components the effect may update","It limits how many times the effect can run in total","It names the hooks used inside the effect","Before each render React compares the dependencies with the previous ones using Object.is and skips the effect if all are equal.")
